1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,236
Episode Seventy-Four covered Spring MVC and REST boundaries.

2
00:00:05,236 --> 00:00:12,217
Most services persist state — Spring Data makes repositories a first-class idea.

3
00:00:12,217 --> 00:00:18,762
JPA and Hibernate remain the common relational stack under Spring Data JPA.

4
00:00:18,762 --> 00:00:25,220
Transactions define consistency boundaries — get them wrong and data lies.

5
00:00:25,220 --> 00:00:31,590
Performance interviews often land on lazy loading and N-plus-one queries.

6
00:00:31,590 --> 00:00:37,786
Today — repositories, entities, transactions, and persistence pitfalls.

7
00:00:37,786 --> 00:00:40,166
Episode Seventy-Five.

8
00:00:40,166 --> 00:00:43,340
Spring Data and Persistence.

9
00:00:43,340 --> 00:00:49,216
Spring Data repositories declare persistence intent as interfaces.

10
00:00:49,216 --> 00:00:55,715
JpaRepository gives CRUD, paging, and sorting with almost no boilerplate.

11
00:00:55,715 --> 00:01:01,859
Query methods derive SQL from method names — findByEmail, existsById.

12
00:01:01,859 --> 00:01:07,646
Query annotation or specs handle complex predicates deliberately.

13
00:01:07,646 --> 00:01:13,522
Keep repositories focused on persistence — not business workflows.

14
00:01:13,522 --> 00:01:19,398
Custom fragments extend repositories when derived queries run out.

15
00:01:19,398 --> 00:01:23,087
Entities map tables to objects — carefully.

16
00:01:23,087 --> 00:01:29,778
Id and GeneratedValue define identity — equals should respect it thoughtfully.

17
00:01:29,778 --> 00:01:35,870
Relationships — OneToMany, ManyToOne — model cardinality and ownership.

18
00:01:35,870 --> 00:01:41,875
FetchType lazy is the default for many associations — for good reason.

19
00:01:41,875 --> 00:01:47,794
DTOs or projections keep API models separate from persistence models.

20
00:01:47,794 --> 00:01:54,400
Schema migrations belong in Flyway or Liquibase — not hope and hibernate-ddl.

21
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:57,898
Transactional marks a consistency boundary.

22
00:01:57,898 --> 00:02:03,105
On a service facade — one use case, one transaction, by default.

23
00:02:03,105 --> 00:02:09,288
Propagation and isolation settings change when nested calls join or suspend.

24
00:02:09,288 --> 00:02:15,146
Read-only transactions can help readers — still measure before assuming.

25
00:02:15,146 --> 00:02:20,922
Checked exceptions do not roll back by default — RuntimeException does.

26
00:02:20,922 --> 00:02:26,210
Keep transactions short — no remote calls while holding DB locks.

27
00:02:26,210 --> 00:02:31,491
The N-plus-one problem — classic interview and production trap.

28
00:02:31,491 --> 00:02:37,192
One query loads parents — N more queries load each child collection.

29
00:02:37,192 --> 00:02:42,474
Symptoms — fine in tests with two rows, painful with thousands.

30
00:02:42,474 --> 00:02:47,420
Fixes — join fetch, entity graphs, or dedicated query DTOs.

31
00:02:47,420 --> 00:02:52,534
Open Session in View can hide the issue until you disable it.

32
00:02:52,534 --> 00:02:57,396
Log SQL in staging — count queries for critical endpoints.

33
00:02:57,396 --> 00:03:00,099
Practical persistence practices.

34
00:03:00,099 --> 00:03:04,577
Index for the queries you run — not for every column.

35
00:03:04,577 --> 00:03:09,223
Paginate at the database — not in memory after findAll.

36
00:03:09,223 --> 00:03:14,207
Optimistic locking with Version prevents silent overwrites.

37
00:03:14,207 --> 00:03:19,698
Separate read models when query shapes diverge from write models.

38
00:03:19,698 --> 00:03:24,598
Measure with realistic data volumes — microbenchmarks lie.

39
00:03:24,598 --> 00:03:26,529
Three common mistakes.

40
00:03:26,529 --> 00:03:32,586
One — calling findAll and filtering in Java — database should filter.

41
00:03:32,586 --> 00:03:38,291
Two — lazy loads in JSON serialization — accidental query storms.

42
00:03:38,291 --> 00:03:43,558
Three — huge transactional methods that call other services.

43
00:03:43,558 --> 00:03:49,527
Also — exposing entities over REST — persistence leaks into clients.

44
00:03:49,527 --> 00:03:55,496
Persistence is a boundary — protect it with clear services and DTOs.

45
00:03:55,496 --> 00:04:01,371
Interview question — how do you avoid N-plus-one with Spring Data JPA?

46
00:04:01,371 --> 00:04:06,827
Detect it — enable SQL logging and watch query counts under load.

47
00:04:06,827 --> 00:04:12,619
Fetch joins or entity graphs load required associations deliberately.

48
00:04:12,619 --> 00:04:17,738
DTO projections query exactly the columns the use case needs.

49
00:04:17,738 --> 00:04:23,026
Avoid Open Session in View masking lazy loads during rendering.

50
00:04:23,026 --> 00:04:28,482
Fix the query shape — do not just raise the connection pool size.

51
00:04:28,482 --> 00:04:32,204
Data is safe — next we lock the doors.

52
00:04:32,204 --> 00:04:35,926
Episode Seventy-Six — Spring Security.

53
00:04:35,926 --> 00:04:42,097
Authentication, authorization, filters, and securing REST APIs.

54
00:04:42,097 --> 00:04:43,468
See you there.
